// Vitest Snapshot v1, https://vitest.dev/guide/snapshot.html

exports[`renderRunbook for DigitalOcean > explains the teardown: no delete protection, snapshot billing and retained backups 1`] = `
"## Tear it down

Run \`opscrew destroy\` on the machine that ran \`opscrew provision\`. It deletes
the Droplet, its firewall, SSH key and app tag (\`opscrew-app:shop\`), and the Cloudflare DNS records and rules OpsCrew created.
It first lists everything it will delete and the data at risk, then asks you to type the Droplet name
(\`shop\`) before anything is deleted. In scripts, pass \`--confirm shop\`.

- **No delete protection:** DigitalOcean has no delete protection for Droplets. Deleting the Droplet in the
  control panel removes it and its data immediately; \`opscrew destroy\` is the safe way, because it shows the plan,
  offers a snapshot and asks for the typed name first.
- **Snapshot first:** because the database lives on the Droplet, OpsCrew takes a DigitalOcean snapshot of the Droplet
  before deleting it (skip it with \`--no-snapshot\`). DigitalOcean keeps daily backups after the Droplet is deleted,
  but each one expires four weeks after it was taken, so the snapshot is the only copy that stays.
- **What remains:** the snapshot, if one was taken (DigitalOcean bills it per GB per month at the rate listed on
  https://www.digitalocean.com/pricing until you delete it in the control panel at https://cloud.digitalocean.com/),
  the daily backups DigitalOcean already took (each is kept for up to four weeks after it was taken, then expires),
  the \`managed-by:opscrew\` tag (other OpsCrew apps in this account may use it), the container images in GitHub
  Container Registry (\`ghcr.io/acme/shop\`), and the files in this repository (\`opscrew/\` and \`.github/workflows/opscrew-deploy.yml\`).
- **What is removed besides the infrastructure:** the GitHub deploy secrets, so the workflow can no longer deploy,
  and the Droplet's host key on this machine. \`opscrew/plan.yaml\` records that the deployment was destroyed.

To deploy again later, run \`opscrew provision\`.
"
`;

exports[`renderRunbook for DigitalOcean > explains the teardown: no delete protection, snapshot billing and retained backups 2`] = `
"## Tear it down

Run \`opscrew destroy\` on the machine that ran \`opscrew provision\`. It deletes
the Droplet, its firewall, SSH key and app tag (\`opscrew-app:shop\`).
It first lists everything it will delete and the data at risk, then asks you to type the Droplet name
(\`shop\`) before anything is deleted. In scripts, pass \`--confirm shop\`.

- **No delete protection:** DigitalOcean has no delete protection for Droplets. Deleting the Droplet in the
  control panel removes it and its data immediately; \`opscrew destroy\` is the safe way, because it shows the plan,
  offers a snapshot and asks for the typed name first.
- **Snapshot:** this app keeps no database on the Droplet and backups are off, so OpsCrew does not take a snapshot
  before deleting the Droplet.
- **What remains:** the snapshot, if one was taken (DigitalOcean bills it per GB per month at the rate listed on
  https://www.digitalocean.com/pricing until you delete it in the control panel at https://cloud.digitalocean.com/),
  the \`managed-by:opscrew\` tag (other OpsCrew apps in this account may use it), the container images in GitHub
  Container Registry (\`ghcr.io/acme/shop\`), and the files in this repository (\`opscrew/\` and \`.github/workflows/opscrew-deploy.yml\`).
- **What is removed besides the infrastructure:** the GitHub deploy secrets, so the workflow can no longer deploy,
  and the Droplet's host key on this machine. \`opscrew/plan.yaml\` records that the deployment was destroyed.

To deploy again later, run \`opscrew provision\`.
"
`;

exports[`renderRunbook for DigitalOcean > names the Droplet, the control panel and USD prices, never Hetzner 1`] = `
"# Runbook: shop

Generated by OpsCrew 0.1.0 from \`opscrew/plan.yaml\`. Run \`opscrew verify\` to refresh it.

## At a glance

| | |
|---|---|
| App | https://shop.example.com |
| Health check | \`/api/health\` |
| Server | shop — DigitalOcean Droplet s-1vcpu-2gb in fra1 |
| IPv4 / IPv6 | kept private behind Cloudflare: \`opscrew status\` shows them |
| Deploys | every push to \`main\` runs \`.github/workflows/opscrew-deploy.yml\` |
| Image | \`ghcr.io/acme/shop\` (GitHub Container Registry) |
| Database | PostgreSQL 17 on the server, data in \`/srv/data/shop\` (see Components); DigitalOcean daily backups are on |
| DNS | Cloudflare, proxied (the server IP is hidden behind Cloudflare) |
| Cost | $15.60 per month (excl. tax, estimate from DigitalOcean's price list of 2026-10-12) |

## How a deploy works

1. You push to \`main\` (or run the "OpsCrew deploy" workflow by hand in GitHub → Actions).
2. GitHub Actions builds the Docker image from your Dockerfile and pushes it to GitHub Container Registry.
3. It copies \`opscrew/runtime/compose.yaml\` (with the services' settings) to \`/srv/apps/shop\` on the server and
   the app's site file to the shared edge in \`/srv/edge\`, writes the app's and the services' environment from the
   deploy secrets, starts the services, runs the release command if the app has one, switches to the new version,
   then restarts the workers and cron jobs with it. A worker that does not stay up sends the app back to the
   previous version.
4. It checks \`/api/health\`. If the app does not answer within three minutes, it restores the previous
   version automatically and the run fails.

## Roll back

- **Fastest:** in GitHub → Actions, open the last successful "OpsCrew deploy" run and choose **Re-run all jobs**.
- **With git:** revert the bad commit and push to \`main\`; the workflow deploys the reverted code.

## Components

The app runs as the Docker Compose project \`shop\` in \`/srv/apps/shop\` on the server. Each component is a
container with its own memory limit, and none publishes a port: only the edge (below) takes web traffic.

| Component | Runs | Memory limit | Data |
|---|---|---|---|
| \`app\` | the web process | 512 MB | |
| \`db\` | PostgreSQL 17 (PostgreSQL) | 512 MB | \`/srv/data/shop/db\` |

\`opscrew status\` shows whether each component runs, and \`docker compose logs <component>\` in the app's folder shows
its log. Change a component's count, memory or version, or turn one off, in \`opscrew/plan.yaml\`, then run
\`opscrew design\` (it checks that everything fits the server and keeps your changes) and \`opscrew provision\`.

## The edge

Web traffic reaches the app through OpsCrew's edge in \`/srv/edge\`, the Compose project \`opscrew-edge\`: Caddy
serves HTTPS and passes each request to kamal-proxy, which switches deploys without downtime. The app's site is
\`/srv/edge/sites/shop.caddy\` (from \`opscrew/runtime/edge/sites/shop.caddy\`). Each deploy copies the edge's files
from \`opscrew/runtime/edge/\` and replaces the edge only with a newer version, so an older app never downgrades it.

- **Your own rules:** \`opscrew/proxy/additions.caddy\` holds this app's own Caddy rules (headers, redirects, rate limits, basic
  authentication). OpsCrew checks them with its rules and with the proxy's own checker before they go live.
- **The proxy:** \`target.edge\` in \`opscrew/plan.yaml\` (\`caddy\` or \`nginx\`). Every app on a server uses the same one;
  it can be switched while the server holds only one OpsCrew app, with a short interruption the plan states.
- **Certificates:** the server serves a Cloudflare origin certificate for this app's hostnames (valid for 15 years)
  and refuses any connection without this app's own client certificate, which only Cloudflare sends for your
  zone. \`opscrew status\` shows both and when they expire; \`opscrew provision\` replaces the origin certificate
  when fewer than 30 days remain. Without the Cloudflare token's SSL and Certificates permission the site keeps
  public certificates, and \`opscrew secure\` lists the gap.

## Connect to the server

\`\`\`bash
ssh -i ~/.opscrew/projects/shop-0123456789ab/deploy_key deploy@$(opscrew status --ip)
# without a global install: ssh -i ~/.opscrew/projects/shop-0123456789ab/deploy_key deploy@$(npx opscrew status --ip)
cd /srv/apps/shop
docker compose ps                 # what is running
docker compose logs -f app        # follow the app's logs
docker compose restart app        # restart the app
\`\`\`

The deploy key is stored only on the machine where you ran \`opscrew provision\` (and as a GitHub Actions
secret). Password login and root login are disabled on the server.

The server's IP address is not written in this repository: Cloudflare's proxy hides it, and a published
address would let anyone go around Cloudflare.
\`opscrew status\` shows the address on the machine that ran \`opscrew provision\` (\`opscrew status --ip\` prints
only the IPv4 address, as used above).

The web Droplet Console in the DigitalOcean control panel cannot log in as root (root SSH login is disabled and
only \`deploy\` may log in); the Recovery Console still works if you ever need it.

## Database backups

DigitalOcean takes a backup of the whole Droplet every day (control panel at https://cloud.digitalocean.com/ → your Droplet → Backups). Each backup expires four weeks after it was taken, also after the Droplet is deleted.

\`\`\`bash
# On the server: dump the database to a file
docker compose exec -T db sh -c 'pg_dump -U "$POSTGRES_USER" "$POSTGRES_DB"' > backup.sql
# Restore a dump
docker compose exec -T db sh -c 'psql -U "$POSTGRES_USER" "$POSTGRES_DB"' < backup.sql
\`\`\`

## Change settings and secrets

- **App environment variables:** run \`opscrew provision\` again; it updates the GitHub deploy secrets. Then re-run the
  deploy workflow so the server picks them up.
- **Server size, location, domain:** run \`opscrew design\` and \`opscrew provision\`. OpsCrew shows the plan and the cost
  change and applies nothing without your approval. Destroying or replacing the server always needs a typed confirmation.
- **Infrastructure code:** \`opscrew/infra/\` is plain Terraform/OpenTofu that you own. You can run it yourself; state is
  kept in \`.opscrew/tfstate/\` on the machine that ran \`opscrew provision\`.

## The secrets recovery copy

\`opscrew/secrets.sops.json\` is an encrypted copy of what OpsCrew keeps for this app on the machine that ran
\`opscrew provision\`: the generated passwords and keys, the deploy key, the infrastructure state and its passphrase,
and OpsCrew's records. Only your age key (or your backup key) opens it, and OpsCrew keeps no copy of the key.
\`opscrew provision\` updates it in its pull request whenever one of those files changes; \`opscrew secrets status\`
shows when it was last updated and whether it is behind.

To manage the deployment from another computer:

\`\`\`bash
git clone <this repository> && cd <it>
opscrew secrets restore --key-file <your age key file>   # or without --key-file, to paste the key unseen
opscrew provision                                        # reuses the restored files: nothing is generated again
\`\`\`

- **Without OpsCrew:** \`SOPS_AGE_KEY_FILE=<your age key file> sops decrypt opscrew/secrets.sops.json\` prints the files.
- **A new key:** \`opscrew secrets rotate --key-out <file>\` writes a new key to that file and proposes the copy,
  encrypted to it, in a pull request. Older commits still open with the old key: if it leaked, change the secrets
  themselves too.

## Moving to another provider

\`opscrew retarget --to <provider>\` moves this app to another provider without losing its data. Run it on the machine
that ran \`opscrew provision\`, with the tokens of both providers set. OpsCrew creates a new server next to the current
one, deploys the app there with the deploy workflow, copies the database and switches the Cloudflare DNS records to
it. Every change shows its plan and waits for your approval; in scripts, each plan stops with its hash and
\`opscrew retarget --approve <hash>\` continues, and \`--yes\` approves storing the GitHub deploy secrets, pushing and
opening the pull request. If a run is interrupted, run \`opscrew retarget\` again: it continues where it stopped, and
\`opscrew status\` shows how far the move got.

- **Maintenance window:** the database is copied while the app is stopped, so the app is offline for a few minutes.
  OpsCrew shows the database size and the expected downtime first and asks before it stops the app (in scripts, pass
  \`--allow-downtime\`). If the copy or the new app fails, the app starts again on the current server.
- **Both servers cost money:** the old server keeps running until you run \`opscrew retarget --finish\` (its app is
  stopped after the DNS switch), and both servers are billed until then. Finish deletes the old server the way
  \`opscrew destroy\` would: it offers a snapshot, asks you to type the app name (\`--confirm shop\` in scripts) and opens
  a pull request with the final infrastructure files.
- **Abort:** \`opscrew retarget --abort\` deletes the new server and puts the app back on the current one, but only
  before the DNS switch. After the switch, the way back is another \`opscrew retarget\`.

## Check for drift

Run \`opscrew drift\` on the machine that ran \`opscrew provision\` (the state lives there). It compares three things:

- **The real infrastructure with the state:** a refresh-only plan shows anything changed outside OpsCrew, for example
  a firewall rule edited in the DigitalOcean control panel or a DNS record changed in Cloudflare.
- **The state with the code:** a normal plan shows changes in \`opscrew/infra/\` that have not been applied yet.
- **The files with the plan:** files under \`opscrew/infra/\` and \`opscrew/runtime/\` that differ from what
  \`opscrew/plan.yaml\` would generate.

\`opscrew drift\` never changes anything and leaves no plan behind that could be applied. It exits with 0 when
everything matches and 1 when something differs (\`--json\` for scripts). To fix drift, run \`opscrew provision\`,
which shows the plan and applies nothing without your approval.

## Security

- Run \`opscrew secure\` for a fresh security report (\`opscrew/SECURITY_REPORT.md\`) and \`opscrew verify\` to check DNS,
  HTTPS, the health endpoint and security headers.
- What OpsCrew set up on the server: a \`deploy\` user with key-only SSH, no root login, fail2ban against brute force,
  automatic security updates, a host firewall (22/80/443) in addition to the DigitalOcean cloud firewall, and Docker log limits.
- Web traffic reaches the server only through Cloudflare; the server's firewall accepts ports 80 and 443 only from
  Cloudflare's IP ranges.

## If something is wrong

1. Open the latest "OpsCrew deploy" run in GitHub → Actions and read the failed step.
2. Run \`opscrew verify\` to see which check fails (DNS, certificate, health endpoint …).
3. On the server, \`docker compose logs --tail 200 app\` usually shows why the app does not start.

## Tear it down

Run \`opscrew destroy\` on the machine that ran \`opscrew provision\`. It deletes
the Droplet, its firewall, SSH key and app tag (\`opscrew-app:shop\`), and the Cloudflare DNS records and rules OpsCrew created.
It first lists everything it will delete and the data at risk, then asks you to type the Droplet name
(\`shop\`) before anything is deleted. In scripts, pass \`--confirm shop\`.

- **No delete protection:** DigitalOcean has no delete protection for Droplets. Deleting the Droplet in the
  control panel removes it and its data immediately; \`opscrew destroy\` is the safe way, because it shows the plan,
  offers a snapshot and asks for the typed name first.
- **Snapshot first:** because the database lives on the Droplet, OpsCrew takes a DigitalOcean snapshot of the Droplet
  before deleting it (skip it with \`--no-snapshot\`). DigitalOcean keeps daily backups after the Droplet is deleted,
  but each one expires four weeks after it was taken, so the snapshot is the only copy that stays.
- **What remains:** the snapshot, if one was taken (DigitalOcean bills it per GB per month at the rate listed on
  https://www.digitalocean.com/pricing until you delete it in the control panel at https://cloud.digitalocean.com/),
  the daily backups DigitalOcean already took (each is kept for up to four weeks after it was taken, then expires),
  the \`managed-by:opscrew\` tag (other OpsCrew apps in this account may use it), the container images in GitHub
  Container Registry (\`ghcr.io/acme/shop\`), and the files in this repository (\`opscrew/\` and \`.github/workflows/opscrew-deploy.yml\`).
- **What is removed besides the infrastructure:** the GitHub deploy secrets, so the workflow can no longer deploy,
  and the Droplet's host key on this machine. \`opscrew/plan.yaml\` records that the deployment was destroyed.

To deploy again later, run \`opscrew provision\`.
"
`;
